hostlog online fetch-tdf --host 169.254.4.10 --output hostlog




This command creates a folder in /hostlog-client/hostlog/tdf/ that follows the naming convention: tdf-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSSSS_vcu

Start Logging:

Replace the folder (underlined and marked in red) with the name of the tdf folder that you created with the first command. 

Change the --max-time-s flag according to how long you want to collect hostlogs

hostlog online start-logging -h 169.254.4.10 -o hostlog --max-time-s 100 --topics  *:*  --to-binary -d ./hostlog/tdf/tdf-20240701_132502048_vcu

hostlog online start-logging -h 169.254.4.10 -o hostlog --max-time-s 100 --topics  *:*  --to-binary -d ./hostlog/tdf/tdf-20240701_132502048_vcu




The logs collected will show up in /hostlog-client/hostlog/bin/

Decoding the Hostlogs:

Before one can analyse the logs we need to decode them. 

First we need to know the software version of the Zenseact module, which can be found in /etc/apps/cs-manifest.yml in HPA

Secondly, we need to retrieve the correct dcf_decoder in https://ara-artifactory.volvocars.biz/artifactory/AD-Zenuity-External-LTS/dcf_decoder/

Searching the last four digits should be sufficient, i.e 2637 in this example.

Run Python file

Download the Python file below for decoding multiple hostlogs at the same time. 

Running the decoder python file requires WSL installed and WSL added to PATH. WSL can be installed by opening powershell/cmd in admin mode and running the command below, and then restarting your computer. WSL should be added to PATH automatically, if not then do it manually.

wsl --install

Then simply execute the python file from Windows (not WSL)

py "batch_decoder_20240603 2.py"

Select the bin folder /hostlog-client/hostlog/bin, select the dcf_decoder that you downloaded before and start the decoding  

This will take some time... The decoded files should appear in /hostlog-client/hostlog/bin/zen_decoded/